# Haryana Districts, Divisions and Administration

> Administrative questions are the safest marks in Haryana GK, and also
> the easiest to lose. The number of districts changed as recently as
> December 2025, seat counts are asked in three houses, and the list of
> Chief Ministers is a favourite sequencing question. This lesson gives
> the dated figures, the six divisions with their districts, the
> machinery of government and the state symbols in one place.

## 1. Districts and Divisions (as of December 2025)

Haryana began in 1966 with seven districts: Ambala, Gurgaon, Hisar,
Jind, Karnal, Mahendragarh and Rohtak. Over time districts were carved
out. **Charkhi Dadri** became the 22nd district on 1 December 2016, and
**Hansi** was declared the **23rd district in December 2025** (notified on
22 December 2025, with the Hansi and Narnaund sub-divisions taken from
Hisar). Always check the date of the question's source, since older books
say 21 or 22.

| Division | Districts |
| --- | --- |
| Ambala | Ambala, Kurukshetra, Panchkula, Yamunanagar |
| Faridabad | Faridabad, Nuh, Palwal |
| Gurugram | Gurugram, Mahendragarh, Rewari |
| Hisar | Hisar, Fatehabad, Jind, Sirsa, Hansi |
| Karnal | Karnal, Kaithal, Panipat |
| Rohtak | Rohtak, Bhiwani, Charkhi Dadri, Jhajjar, Sonipat |

That makes **6 divisions and 23 districts**. Quick recall: Hisar and
Rohtak divisions hold five districts each, Ambala four, and the other
three divisions three each (5+5+4+3+3+3 = 23).

[definition]
**Division** is a tier above the district headed by a Divisional
Commissioner. **District** is headed by a Deputy Commissioner (DC),
who is also the District Magistrate. Below the district come
sub-divisions, tehsils and blocks.
[/definition]

Sub-divisions, tehsils and blocks are notified by the government and the
numbers change; secondary sources list roughly 90 or more revenue tehsils
and about 140 development blocks, but these exact counts vary by date, so
learn only the six divisions and the district count.

**Headquarters trap.** All district headquarters carry the district name
except **Mahendragarh**, whose headquarters is **Narnaul**.
**Mewat** was renamed **Nuh** and **Gurgaon** was renamed **Gurugram** in 2016.

## 2. The Three Houses

| Body | Strength | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Vidhan Sabha (Legislative Assembly) | 90 | 17 seats reserved for Scheduled Castes; unicameral (no Legislative Council); 5-year term |
| Lok Sabha | 10 | 2 reserved seats (Ambala and Sirsa) |
| Rajya Sabha | 5 | Elected by the MLAs |

The **2024 assembly election** (5 October 2024) gave the BJP 48 seats and
the Congress 37, with INLD and independents holding the rest. The
assembly sits at the Palace of Assembly in Chandigarh.

## 3. Governors and Chief Ministers (dated)

Governors in the early years: Dharma Vira (1966-67), B.N. Chakraborty
(1967-76), Harcharan Singh Brar (1977-79), G.D. Tapase (1980-84),
S.M.H. Burney (1984-88). In recent years: Kaptan Singh Solanki
(2014-18), Satyadev Narayan Arya (2018-21), Bandaru Dattatreya
(2021-25) and, **as of 2026, Ashim Kumar Ghosh** (since 21 July 2025).

| Chief Minister | Main tenure |
| --- | --- |
| Bhagwat Dayal Sharma | Nov 1966 - Mar 1967 (first CM) |
| Rao Birender Singh | Mar - Nov 1967 |
| Bansi Lal | 1968-75, again 1986-87 and 1996-99 |
| Banarsi Das Gupta | 1975-77, briefly in 1990 |
| Devi Lal | 1977-79 and 1987-89 |
| Bhajan Lal | 1979-86 and 1991-96 |
| Om Prakash Chautala | 1989-91 in short spells, and 1999-2005 |
| Hukam Singh | Jul 1990 - Mar 1991 |
| Bhupinder Singh Hooda | Mar 2005 - Oct 2014 (two full terms) |
| Manohar Lal Khattar | Oct 2014 - Mar 2024 |
| Nayab Singh Saini | Mar 2024 onward (as of 2026) |

President's Rule has been imposed three times (1967-68, 1977 and 1991).
Bhajan Lal is the longest-serving CM. Hooda served two successive terms (2005-09 and 2009-14).

## 4. Local Government: Panchayati Raj

Rural governance follows the **Haryana Panchayati Raj Act, 1994**, built on
the 73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992). It has three tiers.

| Tier | Level | Count (March 2024) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Gram Panchayat | Village | About 6,225 |
| Panchayat Samiti | Block | 143 |
| Zila Parishad | District | 22 |

Urban areas are run by the Haryana Municipal Corporation Act (1994) and
the Haryana Municipal Act (1973): 11 municipal corporations, 23 municipal
councils and 54 municipal committees as of March 2024. Municipal
corporations include Gurugram, Faridabad, Karnal, Panipat and Rohtak.

[example]
A question: "Which tier of the Panchayati Raj in Haryana operates at the
block level?" Answer: Panchayat Samiti. Village is Gram Panchayat and
district is Zila Parishad.
[/example]

## 5. Courts, Commissions and Symbols

| Institution | Fact |
| --- | --- |
| High Court | Punjab and Haryana High Court, at Chandigarh, common to Punjab, Haryana and the UT |
| HPSC | Haryana Public Service Commission, began 1 November 1966, at Panchkula; recruits Group A and B officers |
| HSSC | Haryana Staff Selection Commission, at Panchkula; recruits mainly Group C and D staff, including through the CET |
| Capital | Chandigarh (shared with Punjab; a Union Territory) |

| State symbol | Haryana's choice |
| --- | --- |
| Animal | Blackbuck |
| Bird | Black francolin (Kala teetar) |
| Tree | Peepal |
| Flower | Lotus |
| Fish | Orangefin labeo |
| Fruit | Mango |

The state motto is *Satyameva Jayate* and the state sport is wrestling.

## 6. Exam Traps

- Hansi is the newest district (December 2025); Charkhi Dadri (2016) was
  the 22nd.
- Narnaul is the HQ of Mahendragarh, not a separate district.
- Rajya Sabha 5, Lok Sabha 10, Vidhan Sabha 90.
- Peepal is the tree and lotus the flower; do not swap them with the
  animal or bird.
- HPSC is for senior state services, while HSSC is mainly for Group C and D posts.

[summary]
Haryana has 6 divisions and, since December 2025, 23 districts (Hansi
joined Charkhi Dadri, 2016). It sends 10 members to the Lok Sabha and 5 to
the Rajya Sabha, and has a 90-seat unicameral Vidhan Sabha. Bhagwat Dayal
Sharma was the first Chief Minister, Dharma Vira the first Governor, and
Nayab Singh Saini is Chief Minister as of 2026. Rural government is
three-tier under the Haryana Panchayati Raj Act, 1994. HPSC and HSSC are at
Panchkula, and the High Court at Chandigarh serves Punjab and Haryana.
[/summary]
